Render a frame as soon as an application commits, instead of on the next beat of the frame rate,
and never more frames in one second than the frame rate.

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86. The lines of context are from that project.

Only for the measurements of step 3 of docs/m1-plan.md: it shows what a compositor gains that
does not wait for a fixed rhythm (docs/measurements/2026-11-16-step3-85fps-gpu-app.md).
The host of step 4 does this in its own compositor.

What it changes:

- A frame the element has asked for is rendered as soon as an application has committed
  something new. If nothing is committed, it goes out after two frame intervals as it is, so
  that the stream does not stop.
- Rendering never starts more often than the frame rate says: at 85 frames a second, the 86th
  frame waits until the first of the last 85 is one second old. Counted where the frames leave
  the element, a second can hold one frame more, because rendering does not always take the
  same time.

The first version of this patch (in the history of this file) rendered on a commit and kept the
old timer as well; it sent 108 to 118 frames a second where 85 were asked for.

Known limit: applications are told to draw their next frame when a frame has been rendered. One
that draws only when told commits right after a frame went out and then waits almost a whole
frame interval for the next. A compositor made for this would tell them a little before the
next frame is due.

Apply and build, next to the unpatched checkout:

    git worktree add ../repo-commit2 1301f86
    cd ../repo-commit2 && git apply <this file>
    cargo build --release -p gst-plugin-wayland-display -p wayland-display-core --features cuda

